---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title =
  "Fidelic: your Salesforce org, running locally. Boots in 2s, breaks on command.";
const description =
  "The failures that page you at 2am — row locks, rate limits, the retry that double-charges — have never run in any sandbox, at any price. Fidelic is a 17.6MB Docker container that loads your real org's schema, runs your real Apex triggers, and produces those failures on command.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  {/* SECTION 1 — the problem */}
  <h1>Salesforce is the only part of your stack with no real CI.</h1>

  <p class="sub">
    Every deploy is "tested" but the failures that make the pager go off have
    never run once: the row lock, the rate limit, the retry that double-charges.
    You can't rehearse them in any sandbox, at any price.
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 2 — the solution */}
  <h2>Fidelic is a Salesforce org you can boot, break, and throw away.</h2>

  <p class="answer">
    A 17.6MB Docker container that loads your real schema and runs your real Apex
    triggers. Point your tests at localhost with same REST responses, same
    trigger side effects, same validation errors. Then flip a switch and get the
    failures no real org allows: row locks, rate limits, latency, on command.
    Wiped clean between tests. As many in parallel as your CI has cores.
  </p>

  <div
    class="terminal"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Terminal: Fidelic boots, classifies each trigger as executed, simulated, or refused with a reason, then returns an UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW error when failure injection is enabled."
  >
    <pre><span class="cmd">docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./mdapi:/mdapi fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi</span>
<span class="dim">fidelic v1.0  ·  booted in 1.9s  ·  image 17.6 MB</span>

<span class="dim">classifying triggers against your org schema …</span>
  <span class="ok">✓ EXECUTE</span>     AccountGuardTrigger     before update — sets Rating when Industry=Technology
  <span class="sim">≈ SIMULATED</span>   CaseTrigger             EventBus.publish → captured, not sent
  <span class="bad">✗ REFUSED</span>     BillingSync.trigger:88  dynamic SOQL — not in the subset

<span class="cmd">export SFEMU_FAULT_LOCK_ON_UPDATE=true</span>   <span class="dim"># break it on command</span>
<span class="cmd">curl -sX PATCH $ORG/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Account/001xx0000…</span>
<span class="bad">&#123; "errorCode": "UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW",</span>
<span class="bad">  "message": "unable to obtain exclusive access to this record" &#125;</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="answer">
    Every trigger is <em>classified</em> at boot: executed, simulated, recorded-only,
    or refused with a reason.
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 3 — receipts, proof, CTA */}
  <div class="receipts">
    <p>
      Verified 66/66 against the public conformance suite
      <a href="https://github.com/fidelic-dev/fidelic-conformance"
        >run the scorecard yourself →</a
      >
    </p>
    <p class="soon">
      66/66 pass · 5 skipped (need a real org) · 2 pending (awaiting real-org
      verification). The skips and pendings are the methodology showing its
      work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="pullquote">
    <p>
      The failure injection alone is worth it. Row locks and API limits are
      untestable in any real org. Fidelic makes them a config flag.
    </p>
    <cite>— Engineer at a major Salesforce partner</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="waitlist" id="waitlist">
    <h2>Get early access</h2>
    <p>
      We onboard a few teams at a time. Real CI for your Salesforce stack starts
      with an email.
    </p>
    <form action="https://formspree.io/f/mkodpozp" method="POST">
      <input
        type="email"
        name="email"
        placeholder="you@company.com"
        aria-label="Email address"
        required
      />
      <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="Fidelic early access" />
      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="_next"
        value="https://www.fidelic.dev/thanks"
      />
      <button class="btn" type="submit">Get early access</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</Base>
